# cmd.exe is happiest with CRLF batch files; without this, a clone made
# with core.autocrlf=false would get LF-only .cmd and %~dp0/label parsing
# can go wrong. Keep them CRLF regardless of the machine's git config.
# PowerShell reads either ending, but these are Windows-only scripts run
# next to the .cmd files; keep them uniform with the batches above.
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# The opposite for shell scripts: these are sourced/executed by bash, and a
# CRLF checkout breaks both the shebang and `source env.sh` (the \r ends up
# inside the last word of every line). Keep them LF regardless of the
# machine's core.autocrlf.
*.sh text eol=lf
